disease-nomenclature anchor\" fails against counterexamples like \"mental retardation,\" which lacks an anchor word yet is still tagged DiseaseClass.\n\nThe confusing part is \"mental retardation\" (correct) and \"ichthyosis\" (discrepancy) appear in the same list in the same sentence with identical syntax, yet the model treated them inconsistently — maybe because \"mental retardation\" reads as a fixed stock disease-term phrase while \"ichthyosis\" was parsed as just another symptom descriptor in a feature list.\n\nNow I'm also noticing a pattern with \"cancer\": bare \"cancer\" gets tagged DiseaseClass as a generic category, while \"ovarian cancer\" gets tagged Modifier when it functions adjectivally before another noun like \"population\" or \"cases\" or \"gene.\" So it seems Modifier applies specifically when a disease term is acting as a prenominal modifier rather than standing as the disease entity itself.\n\nAll five discrepancies share a common trait: they're generic or descriptive condition mentions rather than proper disease names, appearing in explanatory clauses like \"characterized by\" or \"associated with,\" which is likely why the model defaulted to O instead of recognizing them as DiseaseClass.\n\nThe rest check out too — descriptive pathology, clinical lesions, and generic phenotype terms all lack a proper disease name. But \"mental retardation\" breaks the pattern since it's generic yet correctly tagged, so being generic alone doesn't fully explain the failures — it may hinge on whether the term is a fixed multi-word medical heading versus a single-word morphological term or paraphrase.\n\nI try the MeSH heading angle, but that fails too since \"ichthyosis,\" \"exudative retinal detachment,\" and \"cancer\" are all legitimate MeSH headings despite inconsistent handling. I consider instead whether the term functions as a consequence/manifestation within \"characterized by\" or \"results in\" constructions rather than as the named entity itself, but